[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Dynamic routing on router0 (FRRouting/RIP) is down, leaving router0 without routes to external networks. Hosts in Location A still reach their default gateways (router0 subinterfaces eth0.10 = 172.16.10.1 and eth0.20 = 172.16.20.1), but router0 cannot forward beyond Location A.

Mechanism: When Location A hosts send traffic to non-local subnets, router0 generates ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” because it lacks routes to those networks. Evidence: pc4→pc11 (192.168.200.11) returns “From 172.16.10.1 Destination Net Unreachable”; laptop0→pc11 returns the same from 172.16.20.1. On router0, frr.service is failed and there is no /etc/frr/frr.conf, so no RIP is running to learn remote LANs. Router0’s routing table contains only connected networks plus two kernel statics (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.16.1.2) and no route to 192.168.200.0/24; pinging pc11 from router0 yields “Network is unreachable.” In contrast, router1 and router2 have RIP enabled and advertise/learn Location B/C routes, confirming the core is healthy.

Localization: Router0, specifically its FRR daemon and routing table on eth2 (10.16.1.1 toward router1).

Root cause: FRRouting is stopped on router0 and no configuration file is present, so router0 is not participating in RIP and is missing routes to external networks.

Corrective fix: Restore dynamic routing on router0. Create /etc/frr/frr.conf enabling RIP on inter-router links (e.g., “router rip; network 10.0.0.0/8; version 2”) and start frr (systemctl start frr). Alternatively, as a stopgap, add a default route via 10.16.1.2. Verify router0 learns routes (e.g., 192.168.200.0/24) and that Location A hosts can reach Locations B and C. 
=== END ===